{"description":"A process-level map from graph key to an open design, plus the metadata that currently rides on the service — graph_path, content_path, identity, and the write_gen counter the loop nudge reads (service.rs:94-99) — all of which become per-graph rather than per-process.

Opening is ON DEMAND AND BOUNDED. A cold open builds the full-text index and takes seconds on a large design (shared.rs allows 30 seconds for it), so the first request for a graph cannot be treated as cheap, and concurrent first requests for the same graph must COLLAPSE INTO ONE OPEN rather than racing. Eviction has a policy, because each open graph costs a RocksDB instance with its own block cache, file handles and tantivy index: an idle timeout per graph (today's --idle-timeout is per process) plus a cap on simultaneously open graphs, with a stated error when the cap is hit rather than silent thrashing.

Single-writer is untouched: the registry holds N stores and N locks, one lock each. Single-graph mode becomes a registry of one, pre-populated at startup with selection defaulted — not a second code path — so --graph-path, --only-if-present and the latent surface behave exactly as they do today.","is_entry_point":false,"is_exit_point":false,"name":"One process holds many designs: open on demand, bounded, evicted when idle","provenance":"authored","status":"realized","tier":"operational"}
